    "proved": "A sum of normalized independent curve models is an ordinary model, with ideal projection equal to the sum of their ideal models. The induced zero-volume divisorial product locus carries a complete geodesic sum metric, closed geodesically convex analytic ideal locus, unique nearest-point 1-Lipschitz ideal projection and L1 convergence upstairs. Distance to ideal models is the sum of diffuse polar masses.",
    "mechanism": "Induction uses the regularized supremum over the earlier curve factors and the normalized slice on each remaining fiber. Atomic Green potentials produce a finite-mass countable divisor current and compute the ideal envelope. Fibration reduction gives the upstairs projections. Finite products of curve measure cones give completeness and geodesics; rational atomic monomials give analytic closure, fiber integration gives L1 convergence and rank deficiency makes every top-volume comparison zero.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only separated sums of independent curve models on the specified product locus are covered. No unrestricted higher-dimensional model metric, capacity assertion upstairs or nonclosed several-curve extension is claimed."
  },
